<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montego Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montego Bay is a city on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and the capital of the parish of St James. A centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> trade in the colonial period, it was the centre of the 1831–1832 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Christmas Rebellion</a> of enslaved people led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>. In 1947 it hosted the conference on closer association of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> colonies that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> Federation. It is now Jamaica's main tourist centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montego-bay.jpg" alt="Montego Bay" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Don Ramey Logan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montego_Bay_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Montego Bay lies on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, in the parish of St. James. A broad valley runs down to the Caribbean Sea from the Bogue, Kempshot and Salem limestone hills, and the coastal plain is narrow. White sand beaches lie on the east side of the bay, with coral reefs to the north and south and mangrove swamps where the reefs give shelter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> named the bay Golfo de Buen Tiempo, the Fair Weather Gulf. The name Montego Bay is believed to come from the Spanish word manteca, lard, because lard, leather and beef were exported from the port in the Spanish period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> was a Spanish colony from 1511 until 1655, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>'s Western Design expedition drove the Spanish out. The British then created the parish of St. James, and Montego Bay became the island's largest producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane. From the mid-17th century until 1834 the town functioned mainly as a sugar port, and it remained one well into the 20th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Christmas Rebellion</a>, or Baptist War, of 1831 to 1832, the island's last major slave revolt, took place in and around the town. It set estates and plantations on fire and began a broader political push towards emancipation. The British took retribution and many were hanged, among them the leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, who was hanged there in 1832. Sharpe was proclaimed a national hero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1975, and the main square was renamed in his honour.</p><p>Emancipation began on 1 August 1834, with an apprenticeship system that lasted four years, and the town's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry took a hit. It then expanded into exporting bananas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>. The town had lost any city status it held in the colonial period and was proclaimed a city again by act of parliament in 1980, though it remains part of the parish of St. James.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Montego Bay has a cruise terminal and free port, and Sangster International Airport lies within the city limits. Many resorts stand along the nearby coast, some on the grounds of old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations where original buildings and mills remain, such as Rose Hall. Part of the bay, almost sixteen square kilometres including Bogue Island, is set aside as the Montego Bay Marine Park. Most residents are of African descent, with minorities of East Indian and Chinese descent whose ancestors came as indentured servants in the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Montego Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samuel Sharpe, who had planned a peaceful strike, was hanged at <b>Montego Bay</b> in May 1832.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>It was crushed by early 1832, hundreds were executed, and Sharpe was hanged in <b>Montego Bay</b> in May.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Discussions on federation began at <b>Montego Bay</b> in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a></li></ul></section>
